WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.169
<v Tutor>Welcome to Part Two Daily, the Wednesday deep dive.

2
00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:14.449
<v Tutor>This is a test episode for Wednesday the twenty-eighth of October, and today's topic sits right in the middle of this week's shock and kidney block: rhabdomyolysis, and its violent cousin, crush injury.

3
00:00:14.449 --> 00:00:17.779
<v Tutor>It is one of the College's favourite kidney questions.

4
00:00:17.779 --> 00:00:24.762
<v Tutor>It turns up as a list of causes, as a management question, buried inside a trauma scenario, and as a page of biochemistry.

5
00:00:24.762 --> 00:00:26.535
<v Tutor>Here's the running order.

6
00:00:26.535 --> 00:00:35.398
<v Tutor>First, a tutorial: where the syndrome was discovered, the causes by category, why the kidney fails, the electrolyte traps, and what actually changes outcome.

7
00:00:35.398 --> 00:00:39.695
<v Tutor>Then two trials that shape how we give fluid and when we start dialysis.

8
00:00:39.695 --> 00:00:45.120
<v Tutor>Then a real past College question, read word for word, worked by an examiner and a candidate.

9
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.289
<v Tutor>Let's start in London, in the spring of 1941.

10
00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:50.229
<v Tutor>The Blitz.

11
00:00:50.229 --> 00:00:58.796
<v Tutor>People are being dug out of collapsed buildings, sometimes after hours trapped under rubble, and some of them look remarkably well when they reach hospital.

12
00:00:58.796 --> 00:01:00.050
<v Tutor>They're talking.

13
00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.513
<v Tutor>Their pulse is fine.

14
00:01:01.513 --> 00:01:05.117
<v Tutor>A limb is swollen and numb, but they've survived the bombing.

15
00:01:05.117 --> 00:01:08.669
<v Tutor>And then, over the next few days, something strange happens.

16
00:01:08.669 --> 00:01:10.132
<v Tutor>They become shocked.

17
00:01:10.132 --> 00:01:12.483
<v Tutor>Their urine turns dark, almost black.

18
00:01:12.483 --> 00:01:13.528
<v Tutor>It dries up.

19
00:01:13.528 --> 00:01:17.080
<v Tutor>And within about a week, many of them die of kidney failure.

20
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:29.242
<v Tutor>A young doctor at Hammersmith, Eric Bywaters, with his colleague Desmond Beall, wrote these cases up in the British Medical Journal in March 1941, under the title Crush injuries with impairment of renal function.

21
00:01:29.242 --> 00:01:33.412
<v Tutor>It was the first clear description of what we now call crush syndrome.

22
00:01:33.412 --> 00:01:41.431
<v Tutor>Experimental work over the following years linked the kidney injury to a pigment released from damaged muscle, myoglobin, and to acidic urine.

23
00:01:41.431 --> 00:01:45.173
<v Tutor>That's where the tradition of alkalinising the urine was born.

24
00:01:45.173 --> 00:01:50.680
<v Tutor>Hold on to that thought, because eighty years later we're still arguing about whether it works.

25
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.615
<v Tutor>So why does the College love this topic?

26
00:01:53.615 --> 00:02:03.546
<v Tutor>Because it tests whether you can think in categories, because the biochemistry is rich, and because the management has a few traps that separate a safe registrar from a consultant.

27
00:02:03.546 --> 00:02:09.250
<v Tutor>In the question bank there are at least eight past questions built around rhabdomyolysis since 2012.

28
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:18.547
<v Tutor>They come as list five causes, as outline your management, as a crush injury in a trauma stem, and as a biochemistry panel, for example after an aortic aneurysm repair.

29
00:02:18.547 --> 00:02:28.160
<v Tutor>One recent crush question had a pass rate of only forty-four percent, and the examiners said why: candidates gave beautiful answers about blood products and forgot the fluid.

30
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.559
<v Tutor>Start with a definition you can write in one line.

31
00:02:31.559 --> 00:02:37.814
<v Tutor>Rhabdomyolysis is injury to skeletal muscle severe enough to release intracellular contents into the circulation.

32
00:02:37.814 --> 00:02:47.017
<v Tutor>Biochemically, it's usually defined as a creatine kinase more than five times the upper limit of normal, which in practice means above about one thousand units per litre.

33
00:02:47.017 --> 00:02:52.497
<v Tutor>The classic triad is muscle pain, weakness and dark urine, but most patients don't have all three.

34
00:02:52.497 --> 00:02:55.755
<v Tutor>Many of yours will be sedated, so the triad is useless.

35
00:02:55.755 --> 00:02:58.960
<v Tutor>You find it because you thought of it and sent the CK.

36
00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.458
<v Tutor>Now the causes.

37
00:03:00.458 --> 00:03:07.998
<v Tutor>When the College asks you to list five causes, it gives half a mark per cause, and it won't give extra credit for three causes under the same heading.

38
00:03:07.998 --> 00:03:10.527
<v Tutor>It also wants examples, not just the heading.

39
00:03:10.527 --> 00:03:15.347
<v Tutor>So the winning move is five causes from five different categories, each with a named example.

40
00:03:15.347 --> 00:03:18.640
<v Tutor>Let me give you a framework you can reproduce under pressure.

41
00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:28.527
<v Tutor>First, physical injury and compression: crush injury, prolonged immobilisation after a fall or an overdose, prolonged surgery in one position.

42
00:03:28.527 --> 00:03:36.180
<v Tutor>Second, exertion and excessive muscle activity: extreme exercise, seizures, status epilepticus, severe agitation.

43
00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:45.098
<v Tutor>Third, ischaemia: compartment syndrome, arterial occlusion, and reperfusion after an aortic aneurysm repair or a revascularised limb.

44
00:03:45.098 --> 00:03:52.751
<v Tutor>Fourth, temperature: heat stroke, malignant hyperthermia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and severe hypothermia.

45
00:03:52.751 --> 00:04:03.440
<v Tutor>Fifth, drugs and toxins: statins, especially combined with fibrates, alcohol, cocaine and amphetamines, propofol infusion syndrome, colchicine, and snake venoms.

46
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:09.512
<v Tutor>Sixth, infection: influenza, COVID, Legionella, streptococcal and staphylococcal sepsis.

47
00:04:09.512 --> 00:04:18.999
<v Tutor>Seventh, metabolic and electrolyte causes: hypokalaemia, hypophosphataemia, hyperosmolar states and diabetic emergencies, and thyroid disease.

48
00:04:18.999 --> 00:04:28.360
<v Tutor>And finally, inherited and inflammatory causes: McArdle disease, carnitine palmitoyltransferase deficiency, and the inflammatory myositides.

49
00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:31.118
<v Tutor>Let's test that while it's fresh.

50
00:04:31.118 --> 00:04:37.385
<v Tutor>Name five causes of rhabdomyolysis, one from each of five different categories, each with an example.

51
00:04:37.385 --> 00:04:38.880
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

52
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:55.935
<v Tutor>A strong list would be: crush injury, status epilepticus, compartment syndrome after vascular surgery, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and a statin combined with a fibrate.

53
00:04:55.935 --> 00:04:59.960
<v Tutor>Five categories, five examples, two and a half marks banked.

54
00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:01.467
<v Tutor>Now the why.

55
00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:04.123
<v Tutor>Why does damaged muscle kill the kidney?

56
00:05:04.123 --> 00:05:08.328
<v Tutor>Think of three hits on the tubule, plus a fourth on the circulation.

57
00:05:08.328 --> 00:05:10.376
<v Tutor>The first hit is obstruction.

58
00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:14.858
<v Tutor>Myoglobin is small, about seventeen kilodaltons, so it's freely filtered.

59
00:05:14.858 --> 00:05:21.885
<v Tutor>In acidic, concentrated urine it precipitates with Tamm-Horsfall protein and forms casts that block the distal tubules.

60
00:05:21.885 --> 00:05:24.209
<v Tutor>The second hit is direct toxicity.

61
00:05:24.209 --> 00:05:30.019
<v Tutor>The haem iron in myoglobin drives oxidative injury, with lipid peroxidation of the tubular cells.

62
00:05:30.019 --> 00:05:32.343
<v Tutor>The third hit is vasoconstriction.

63
00:05:32.343 --> 00:05:36.383
<v Tutor>Free haem mops up nitric oxide, and the renal vessels clamp down.

64
00:05:36.383 --> 00:05:38.762
<v Tutor>And the fourth hit is hypovolaemia.

65
00:05:38.762 --> 00:05:42.359
<v Tutor>Injured muscle swells and sequesters extracellular fluid.

66
00:05:42.359 --> 00:05:45.403
<v Tutor>A crushed pair of legs can hide several litres.

67
00:05:45.403 --> 00:05:51.600
<v Tutor>That's why these patients become shocked after they've been rescued, which is exactly what Bywaters saw.

68
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.484
<v Tutor>That mechanism explains the treatment.

69
00:05:54.484 --> 00:05:58.803
<v Tutor>More flow through the tubule dilutes the myoglobin and washes out casts.

70
00:05:58.803 --> 00:06:03.446
<v Tutor>Alkaline urine should, in theory, reduce precipitation and the iron chemistry.

71
00:06:03.446 --> 00:06:07.873
<v Tutor>Restoring volume reverses the vasoconstriction and the prerenal component.

72
00:06:07.873 --> 00:06:11.005
<v Tutor>So the physiology says fluid, and possibly alkali.

73
00:06:11.005 --> 00:06:13.920
<v Tutor>Keep that in mind when we get to the evidence.

74
00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:15.633
<v Tutor>Investigations.

75
00:06:15.633 --> 00:06:21.573
<v Tutor>The CK rises within hours, peaks at roughly one to three days, and then falls over several days.

76
00:06:21.573 --> 00:06:27.340
<v Tutor>Myoglobin is cleared within hours, so a normal serum myoglobin doesn't exclude the diagnosis.

77
00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:30.082
<v Tutor>The bedside trick is the urine dipstick.

78
00:06:30.082 --> 00:06:36.535
<v Tutor>It reads positive for blood, because the test detects haem, but the microscopy shows few or no red cells.

79
00:06:36.535 --> 00:06:41.103
<v Tutor>That combination should make you think of myoglobin or free haemoglobin.

80
00:06:41.103 --> 00:06:45.900
<v Tutor>Then there's the electrolyte panel: potassium, calcium, phosphate and urate.

81
00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:51.154
<v Tutor>Check creatinine, which can rise faster than in other causes of acute kidney injury.

82
00:06:51.154 --> 00:06:57.950
<v Tutor>Check for a high anion gap metabolic acidosis, and for coagulopathy and disseminated intravascular coagulation.

83
00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.205
<v Tutor>Measure compartment pressures if a limb is tense.

84
00:07:01.205 --> 00:07:08.800
<v Tutor>And then the cause-directed tests: temperature, a drug screen, thyroid function, respiratory viral swabs, and blood cultures.

85
00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:10.631
<v Tutor>One exam trap here.

86
00:07:10.631 --> 00:07:18.688
<v Tutor>In the 2020 question, the examiners noted that some candidates listed CT scans and exploratory surgery when asked for laboratory investigations.

87
00:07:18.688 --> 00:07:21.921
<v Tutor>Those aren't laboratory tests, and they earn nothing.

88
00:07:21.921 --> 00:07:24.200
<v Tutor>Answer the question that was asked.

89
00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:29.370
<v Tutor>Now the electrolytes, and this is where the calcium and phosphate story gets interesting.

90
00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:35.811
<v Tutor>Earlier this month you dropped marks on hypophosphataemia and hypocalcaemia, so here's where those two meet a real patient.

91
00:07:35.811 --> 00:07:39.499
<v Tutor>Dying muscle cells release phosphate, so the serum phosphate rises.

92
00:07:39.499 --> 00:07:47.612
<v Tutor>Calcium then binds phosphate and deposits as calcium phosphate in the damaged muscle, so the serum calcium falls, sometimes quite low, in the first few days.

93
00:07:47.612 --> 00:07:53.955
<v Tutor>Then, during recovery, that deposited calcium is mobilised again, and a proportion of patients swing into hypercalcaemia.

94
00:07:53.955 --> 00:07:57.200
<v Tutor>That's especially likely if they were given calcium early.

95
00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.156
<v Tutor>Here's the question that catches people.

96
00:08:00.156 --> 00:08:03.298
<v Tutor>Early in rhabdomyolysis the ionised calcium is low.

97
00:08:03.298 --> 00:08:04.895
<v Tutor>Should you replace it?

98
00:08:04.895 --> 00:08:06.280
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

99
00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:13.786
<v Tutor>Usually not.

100
00:08:13.786 --> 00:08:23.189
<v Tutor>Treat the low calcium only if it's causing harm: tetany, seizures, arrhythmia, or hyperkalaemia with ECG changes, where calcium is part of membrane stabilisation.

101
00:08:23.189 --> 00:08:29.107
<v Tutor>Otherwise, extra calcium feeds the deposits in the muscle and sets up rebound hypercalcaemia later.

102
00:08:29.107 --> 00:08:31.209
<v Tutor>And remember the mirror image.

103
00:08:31.209 --> 00:08:39.394
<v Tutor>Hypophosphataemia isn't only a consequence of illness, it's a cause of rhabdomyolysis, classically in refeeding and in alcohol use disorder.

104
00:08:39.394 --> 00:08:43.045
<v Tutor>Low phosphate as a cause, high phosphate as a consequence.

105
00:08:43.045 --> 00:08:46.640
<v Tutor>That contrast is worth a mark in a biochemistry question.

106
00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:57.196
<v Tutor>Hyperkalaemia is the electrolyte that kills, and it can arrive suddenly, especially when a crushed limb is freed and the potassium-rich blood from that limb returns to the circulation.

107
00:08:57.196 --> 00:09:04.972
<v Tutor>In disaster medicine, that's why people talk about the smile of death: the trapped patient who's chatting, is extricated, and then arrests.

108
00:09:04.972 --> 00:09:11.320
<v Tutor>The European renal association's field guidance says to expect hyperkalaemia at all times and to treat it early.

109
00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:14.490
<v Tutor>How do you predict who'll need dialysis?

110
00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:22.744
<v Tutor>The McMahon score, published in 2013, combines age, sex, the cause, and the initial creatinine, calcium, phosphate, bicarbonate and CK.

111
00:09:22.744 --> 00:09:28.227
<v Tutor>Its main use is identifying low-risk patients who are unlikely to need dialysis or die.

112
00:09:28.227 --> 00:09:35.787
<v Tutor>The CK alone is a poor predictor, though acute kidney injury becomes more likely as the CK climbs past about five thousand.

113
00:09:35.787 --> 00:09:38.096
<v Tutor>Name the score and what it does.

114
00:09:38.096 --> 00:09:40.520
<v Tutor>You won't be asked its weightings.

115
00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.162
<v Tutor>Now management.

116
00:09:42.162 --> 00:09:44.809
<v Tutor>Use a structure the examiners can follow.

117
00:09:44.809 --> 00:09:47.725
<v Tutor>One: treat immediate threats to life and limb.

118
00:09:47.725 --> 00:09:51.182
<v Tutor>Two: stop the ongoing muscle injury and treat the cause.

119
00:09:51.182 --> 00:09:53.666
<v Tutor>Three: protect the kidney with volume.

120
00:09:53.666 --> 00:09:56.421
<v Tutor>Four: manage the electrolytes and acidosis.

121
00:09:56.421 --> 00:09:59.986
<v Tutor>Five: renal replacement therapy for the usual indications.

122
00:09:59.986 --> 00:10:02.200
<v Tutor>And six: watch for complications.

123
00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:04.368
<v Tutor>Immediate threats first.

124
00:10:04.368 --> 00:10:13.209
<v Tutor>Hyperkalaemia gets calcium for membrane stabilisation, insulin with dextrose, nebulised salbutamol, bicarbonate if acidotic, and dialysis if refractory.

125
00:10:13.209 --> 00:10:14.867
<v Tutor>Then the compartments.

126
00:10:14.867 --> 00:10:21.110
<v Tutor>A tense, painful compartment with pain on passive stretch is compartment syndrome until proven otherwise.

127
00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:33.322
<v Tutor>A compartment pressure above about thirty millimetres of mercury, or a perfusion gap between diastolic pressure and compartment pressure of less than thirty, should prompt an urgent surgical review for fasciotomy.

128
00:10:33.322 --> 00:10:40.560
<v Tutor>Then stop the cause: cool the hyperthermic patient, stop the statin or the propofol, and treat the seizures and the sepsis.

129
00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.220
<v Tutor>Then the kidney.

130
00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:46.839
<v Tutor>Early, generous isotonic crystalloid is the one intervention everyone agrees on.

131
00:10:46.839 --> 00:10:51.773
<v Tutor>In a crush victim, the field guidance from the European renal association is specific.

132
00:10:51.773 --> 00:10:57.232
<v Tutor>Start isotonic saline at about one litre an hour in adults, ideally before the limb is released.

133
00:10:57.232 --> 00:11:00.329
<v Tutor>Aim for three to six litres in the first six hours.

134
00:11:00.329 --> 00:11:03.215
<v Tutor>Avoid potassium-containing fluids in the field.

135
00:11:03.215 --> 00:11:05.472
<v Tutor>Then keep reassessing for overload.

136
00:11:05.472 --> 00:11:08.149
<v Tutor>In the ICU, titrate to a high urine output.

137
00:11:08.149 --> 00:11:20.327
<v Tutor>The College's model answer to the recent crush question talked about crystalloid at around four hundred millilitres an hour, up to a litre an hour, aiming for a urine output of one to three millilitres per kilogram per hour.

138
00:11:20.327 --> 00:11:22.217
<v Tutor>And here's the safety point.

139
00:11:22.217 --> 00:11:27.833
<v Tutor>If the patient is anuric despite resuscitation, stop pushing fluid, because you'll just drown them.

140
00:11:27.833 --> 00:11:29.880
<v Tutor>That's a dialysis decision now.

141
00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:33.696
<v Tutor>This is exactly where that recent crush question was lost.

142
00:11:33.696 --> 00:11:37.734
<v Tutor>In your attempt on the twelfth of October, you scored four and a half.

143
00:11:37.734 --> 00:11:43.635
<v Tutor>The marks that slipped away were the crush injury itself and a concrete fluid strategy for rhabdomyolysis.

144
00:11:43.635 --> 00:11:46.793
<v Tutor>Three days later you wrote it again and scored seven.

145
00:11:46.793 --> 00:11:48.864
<v Tutor>So you've already fixed it once.

146
00:11:48.864 --> 00:11:55.800
<v Tutor>The examiners' comment is the lesson: when a question says blood and fluid, give fluid equal weight, with a rate and a target.

147
00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.303
<v Tutor>Now bicarbonate and mannitol.

148
00:11:58.303 --> 00:12:00.975
<v Tutor>This is the eighty-year-old Blitz idea.

149
00:12:00.975 --> 00:12:09.161
<v Tutor>The rationale is sound: alkaline urine to reduce cast formation, and mannitol for osmotic diuresis and perhaps scavenging free radicals.

150
00:12:09.161 --> 00:12:12.345
<v Tutor>But there's no randomised trial showing benefit.

151
00:12:12.345 --> 00:12:21.042
<v Tutor>The best-known clinical study, by Brown and colleagues in the Journal of Trauma in 2004, looked at trauma patients with a CK above five thousand.

152
00:12:21.042 --> 00:12:26.045
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate and mannitol made no difference to renal failure, dialysis or death.

153
00:12:26.045 --> 00:12:27.978
<v Tutor>And bicarbonate has costs.

154
00:12:27.978 --> 00:12:32.241
<v Tutor>It worsens ionised hypocalcaemia and can cause metabolic alkalosis.

155
00:12:32.241 --> 00:12:39.859
<v Tutor>In the 2020 question, the College said alkalinisation, mannitol and frusemide were optional and attracted no marks either way.

156
00:12:39.859 --> 00:12:43.440
<v Tutor>So mention them, say the evidence is weak, and move on.

157
00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:45.193
<v Tutor>Which crystalloid?

158
00:12:45.193 --> 00:12:48.211
<v Tutor>Historically, saline, because it has no potassium.

159
00:12:48.211 --> 00:12:51.645
<v Tutor>But saline brings chloride and a hyperchloraemic acidosis.

160
00:12:51.645 --> 00:12:56.224
<v Tutor>Balanced solutions contain about four to five millimoles per litre of potassium.

161
00:12:56.224 --> 00:13:01.844
<v Tutor>That's less than the potassium already in the patient's plasma, so they don't meaningfully raise it.

162
00:13:01.844 --> 00:13:05.278
<v Tutor>In the ICU, a balanced crystalloid is entirely reasonable.

163
00:13:05.278 --> 00:13:09.805
<v Tutor>In the field, before you know the potassium, the crush guidance prefers saline.

164
00:13:09.805 --> 00:13:13.760
<v Tutor>Being able to say why both views exist is a consultant-level answer.

165
00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:22.026
<v Tutor>Renal replacement therapy follows the usual indications: refractory hyperkalaemia, acidosis, fluid overload, and uraemic complications.

166
00:13:22.026 --> 00:13:25.217
<v Tutor>Removing myoglobin isn't an indication on its own.

167
00:13:25.217 --> 00:13:31.212
<v Tutor>Myoglobin is cleared by some high cut-off membranes, but that's never been shown to improve outcomes.

168
00:13:31.212 --> 00:13:34.348
<v Tutor>Say that explicitly; it's a common discriminator.

169
00:13:34.348 --> 00:13:50.080
<v Tutor>And list the complications to watch for: acute kidney injury, hyperkalaemic arrest, compartment syndrome, disseminated intravascular coagulation, hypocalcaemia then rebound hypercalcaemia, and, in crush, the injuries and therapies that came with it, such as massive transfusion.

170
00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.660
<v Tutor>Let's do one more before the trials.

171
00:13:52.660 --> 00:14:00.240
<v Tutor>A crush victim has a potassium of seven point two with peaked T waves and a urine output of ten millilitres an hour despite four litres of fluid.

172
00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.272
<v Tutor>What are your next three actions?

173
00:14:02.272 --> 00:14:03.560
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

174
00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:17.405
<v Tutor>First, ten millilitres of ten percent calcium chloride, or thirty of calcium gluconate, to stabilise the membrane.

175
00:14:17.405 --> 00:14:22.776
<v Tutor>Second, insulin and dextrose with salbutamol, to shift potassium into the cells.

176
00:14:22.776 --> 00:14:30.709
<v Tutor>Third, stop escalating fluid and arrange urgent dialysis, because this is refractory hyperkalaemia in an oliguric patient.

177
00:14:30.709 --> 00:14:34.920
<v Tutor>If you also said check the compartments, that's a bonus mark.

178
00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:37.240
<v Tutor>Three numbers to remember.

179
00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:45.880
<v Tutor>One: a CK more than five times the upper limit of normal defines rhabdomyolysis, and kidney injury becomes more likely above about five thousand.

180
00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:54.294
<v Tutor>Two: in crush, isotonic fluid at about one litre an hour, three to six litres in the first six hours, started before extrication if possible.

181
00:14:54.294 --> 00:15:00.280
<v Tutor>Three: a compartment perfusion gap of less than thirty millimetres of mercury means a surgeon now.

182
00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:08.040
<v Tutor>Two trials shape what you do for these patients, one about fluid and one about dialysis.

183
00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:15.629
<v Tutor>First, PLUS, an Australian and New Zealand trial published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2022, led by Simon Finfer.

184
00:15:15.629 --> 00:15:26.226
<v Tutor>It randomised five thousand and thirty-seven critically ill adults needing fluid resuscitation to a balanced multi-electrolyte solution, Plasma-Lyte 148, or to zero point nine percent saline.

185
00:15:26.226 --> 00:15:34.480
<v Tutor>The primary outcome was ninety-day mortality: twenty-one point eight percent with the balanced solution, twenty-two point zero percent with saline.

186
00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:35.651
<v Tutor>No difference.

187
00:15:35.651 --> 00:15:41.402
<v Tutor>There was also no significant difference in new renal replacement therapy or in acute kidney injury.

188
00:15:41.402 --> 00:15:50.295
<v Tutor>The criticism is that many patients had received fluid before enrolment, and the doses given in the trial were moderate, which may have diluted any difference.

189
00:15:50.295 --> 00:15:58.017
<v Tutor>How it changed practice: either fluid is acceptable for most patients, so choose on the patient's chloride, acid–base status and context.

190
00:15:58.017 --> 00:16:08.720
<v Tutor>In an exam answer you'd say: in the PLUS trial of over five thousand ICU patients, balanced crystalloid didn't reduce ninety-day mortality compared with saline, so the choice is individualised.

191
00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:17.458
<v Tutor>Second, STARRT-AKI, also in the New England Journal of Medicine, in 2020, from an international group including Australian and New Zealand sites.

192
00:16:17.458 --> 00:16:34.134
<v Tutor>It randomised three thousand and nineteen patients with severe acute kidney injury, but without an urgent indication for dialysis, to accelerated renal replacement therapy, started within twelve hours, or to a standard strategy that waited for a conventional indication or persistent kidney injury.

193
00:16:34.134 --> 00:16:39.748
<v Tutor>Ninety-day mortality was forty-three point nine percent versus forty-three point seven percent.

194
00:16:39.748 --> 00:16:40.783
<v Tutor>No benefit.

195
00:16:40.783 --> 00:16:43.126
<v Tutor>But look at the secondary outcomes.

196
00:16:43.126 --> 00:16:51.465
<v Tutor>Among survivors, dependence on dialysis at ninety days was higher in the accelerated group, ten point four percent versus six point zero percent.

197
00:16:51.465 --> 00:16:59.639
<v Tutor>And adverse events were more common with early dialysis, twenty-three versus sixteen and a half percent, mostly hypotension and low phosphate.

198
00:16:59.639 --> 00:17:03.890
<v Tutor>The criticism is that it can't tell you when waiting becomes too long.

199
00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:08.577
<v Tutor>A later trial, AKIKI two, suggested that delaying even further may cause harm.

200
00:17:08.577 --> 00:17:13.373
<v Tutor>The take-home message: don't start dialysis just because the creatinine is high.

201
00:17:13.373 --> 00:17:15.280
<v Tutor>Start it for an indication.

202
00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:30.140
<v Tutor>In your rhabdomyolysis answer, that's exactly the line: renal replacement therapy for standard indications, supported by STARRT-AKI, which showed no survival benefit from accelerated initiation and more dialysis dependence.

203
00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:33.444
<v Tutor>Here's a quick check on the trials.

204
00:17:33.444 --> 00:17:40.264
<v Tutor>In STARRT-AKI, what happened to dialysis dependence at ninety days with an accelerated start?

205
00:17:40.264 --> 00:17:42.020
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

206
00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:52.319
<v Tutor>It was higher: ten point four percent versus six point zero percent among survivors.

207
00:17:52.319 --> 00:17:56.260
<v Tutor>Earlier wasn't better, and it may have been worse for the kidneys.

208
00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:58.757
<v Examiner>Good morning.

209
00:17:58.757 --> 00:18:01.788
<v Examiner>This is from the 2020 first sitting, question one.

210
00:18:01.788 --> 00:18:03.878
<v Examiner>I'll read it exactly as written.

211
00:18:03.878 --> 00:18:06.908
<v Examiner>With regards to rhabdomyolysis in the ICU patient.

212
00:18:06.908 --> 00:18:07.692
<v Examiner>Part a.

213
00:18:07.692 --> 00:18:08.998
<v Examiner>List five causes.

214
00:18:08.998 --> 00:18:10.670
<v Examiner>Twenty percent of marks.

215
00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:11.454
<v Examiner>Part b.

216
00:18:11.454 --> 00:18:18.926
<v Examiner>What are the important features in the history and clinical examination, and what specific laboratory investigations would you request?

217
00:18:18.926 --> 00:18:20.598
<v Examiner>Thirty percent of marks.

218
00:18:20.598 --> 00:18:21.382
<v Examiner>Part c.

219
00:18:21.382 --> 00:18:23.002
<v Examiner>Outline the management.

220
00:18:23.002 --> 00:18:24.621
<v Examiner>Fifty percent of marks.

221
00:18:24.621 --> 00:18:25.980
<v Examiner>Start with part a.

222
00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:27.615
<v Candidate>Five causes.

223
00:18:27.615 --> 00:18:31.473
<v Candidate>Crush injury, for example a patient trapped under a vehicle.

224
00:18:31.473 --> 00:18:33.004
<v Candidate>Status epilepticus.

225
00:18:33.004 --> 00:18:36.976
<v Candidate>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, for example after haloperidol.

226
00:18:36.976 --> 00:18:39.983
<v Candidate>Statin toxicity, particularly with a fibrate.

227
00:18:39.983 --> 00:18:43.160
<v Candidate>And compartment syndrome after vascular surgery.

228
00:18:43.660 --> 00:18:45.588
<v Examiner>Five causes, but count your categories.

229
00:18:45.588 --> 00:18:48.254
<v Examiner>Compartment syndrome and crush injury sit close together.

230
00:18:48.254 --> 00:18:50.100
<v Examiner>Give me one that's clearly different.

231
00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:54.042
<v Candidate>Infection, for example influenza or Legionella.

232
00:18:54.042 --> 00:18:58.360
<v Candidate>Or a metabolic cause, such as hypophosphataemia in refeeding.

233
00:18:58.860 --> 00:18:59.743
<v Examiner>Better.

234
00:18:59.743 --> 00:19:00.626
<v Examiner>Part b.

235
00:19:00.626 --> 00:19:04.100
<v Examiner>History, examination and laboratory investigations.

236
00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:20.985
<v Candidate>In the history: trauma or a long lie, seizures, exertion, heat exposure, drugs including statins, antipsychotics, propofol, alcohol and stimulants, recent viral illness, muscle pain and dark urine, and baseline kidney function.

237
00:19:20.985 --> 00:19:31.373
<v Candidate>On examination: tender, swollen muscle compartments, weakness, temperature, signs of volume depletion, pressure areas, and peripheral pulses.

238
00:19:31.373 --> 00:19:45.178
<v Candidate>Investigations: CK, creatinine and urea, potassium, calcium, phosphate, urate, a blood gas for the acidosis and anion gap, a coagulation screen for DIC, and a urine dipstick with microscopy.

239
00:19:45.178 --> 00:19:48.734
<v Candidate>The dipstick shows blood with no red cells.

240
00:19:48.734 --> 00:19:55.078
<v Candidate>Then cause-directed tests, such as a drug screen, thyroid function and viral swabs.

241
00:19:55.078 --> 00:19:59.680
<v Candidate>I'd also get a CT of the limb to look for muscle necrosis.

242
00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:01.121
<v Examiner>Stop there.

243
00:20:01.121 --> 00:20:03.942
<v Examiner>The question asked for laboratory investigations.

244
00:20:03.942 --> 00:20:05.180
<v Examiner>Is a CT scan one?

245
00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:06.796
<v Candidate>No, it isn't.

246
00:20:06.796 --> 00:20:07.912
<v Candidate>I'll drop it.

247
00:20:07.912 --> 00:20:12.800
<v Candidate>I'd add serial CK and potassium, because the trend matters more than a single value.

248
00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:14.001
<v Examiner>Good.

249
00:20:14.001 --> 00:20:15.456
<v Examiner>Part c, management.

250
00:20:15.456 --> 00:20:17.180
<v Examiner>Half the marks are here.

251
00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:19.123
<v Candidate>I'd structure it.

252
00:20:19.123 --> 00:20:21.027
<v Candidate>First, immediate threats.

253
00:20:21.027 --> 00:20:30.895
<v Candidate>Treat hyperkalaemia with calcium, insulin and dextrose, and salbutamol, and check the compartments, measuring pressures with early surgical review if one is tense.

254
00:20:30.895 --> 00:20:37.705
<v Candidate>Second, treat the cause, for example stopping the statin, or cooling and dantrolene in malignant hyperthermia.

255
00:20:37.705 --> 00:20:40.937
<v Candidate>Third, protect the kidneys with early IV fluids.

256
00:20:40.937 --> 00:20:45.438
<v Candidate>Fourth, correct the electrolytes, including replacing the low calcium.

257
00:20:45.438 --> 00:20:47.400
<v Candidate>Fifth, dialysis if needed.

258
00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:49.058
<v Examiner>Two problems.

259
00:20:49.058 --> 00:20:52.367
<v Examiner>Your fluid plan has no fluid, no rate and no target.

260
00:20:52.367 --> 00:20:55.180
<v Examiner>How much, and how do you know it's working?

261
00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:59.321
<v Candidate>Isotonic crystalloid, started early and given generously.

262
00:20:59.321 --> 00:21:05.259
<v Candidate>In a crush patient, about one litre an hour initially, three to six litres in the first six hours.

263
00:21:05.259 --> 00:21:12.430
<v Candidate>Then titrate to a urine output of around one to three millilitres per kilogram per hour, reassessing often for overload.

264
00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:17.640
<v Candidate>If they're anuric despite adequate volume, I stop escalating fluid and plan dialysis.

265
00:21:18.140 --> 00:21:19.300
<v Examiner>And the calcium?

266
00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:27.129
<v Candidate>I'd only replace calcium if it's symptomatic, if there's an arrhythmia, or if there's hyperkalaemia with ECG changes.

267
00:21:27.129 --> 00:21:32.757
<v Candidate>Calcium deposits in the damaged muscle and can cause rebound hypercalcaemia in recovery.

268
00:21:32.757 --> 00:21:36.040
<v Candidate>I'd tolerate a moderately low calcium otherwise.

269
00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:38.660
<v Examiner>What about bicarbonate and mannitol?

270
00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:43.985
<v Candidate>There's a physiological rationale, but no randomised evidence of benefit.

271
00:21:43.985 --> 00:21:52.980
<v Candidate>A retrospective trauma study by Brown in 2004 showed no reduction in renal failure, dialysis or death in patients with a CK over five thousand.

272
00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:56.256
<v Candidate>Bicarbonate also worsens ionised hypocalcaemia.

273
00:21:56.256 --> 00:21:58.520
<v Candidate>I wouldn't use them routinely.

274
00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:00.602
<v Examiner>And dialysis.

275
00:22:00.602 --> 00:22:02.260
<v Examiner>When, and why?

276
00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:10.798
<v Candidate>For standard indications: refractory hyperkalaemia, severe acidosis, fluid overload, or uraemic complications.

277
00:22:10.798 --> 00:22:15.158
<v Candidate>Not to clear myoglobin, which hasn't been shown to help.

278
00:22:15.158 --> 00:22:18.292
<v Candidate>And not early just for the creatinine.

279
00:22:18.292 --> 00:22:25.240
<v Candidate>STARRT-AKI showed no survival benefit from an accelerated start, and more dialysis dependence.

280
00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:27.886
<v Examiner>Here's how that would be marked.

281
00:22:27.886 --> 00:22:36.737
<v Examiner>Part a earns half a mark per cause, so five causes from five categories with examples gets the full two marks, and you got there after I made you change one.

282
00:22:36.737 --> 00:22:42.102
<v Examiner>Part b earns marks for a targeted history, a focused examination, and laboratory tests only.

283
00:22:42.102 --> 00:22:46.876
<v Examiner>The CT scan earned nothing, and in the written paper I wouldn't have stopped you.

284
00:22:46.876 --> 00:22:48.914
<v Examiner>Part c is where the marks are.

285
00:22:48.914 --> 00:23:02.755
<v Examiner>They come for a clear structure: immediate threats including hyperkalaemia and compartment syndrome, stopping the cause, early aggressive isotonic fluid with a rate and a target, cautious electrolyte correction, and dialysis for standard indications.

286
00:23:02.755 --> 00:23:08.280
<v Examiner>Your first pass gave fluid with no numbers and replaced the calcium, which would have cost you.

287
00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:12.143
<v Examiner>After probing, it's a comfortable pass, around seven out of ten.

288
00:23:12.143 --> 00:23:18.580
<v Examiner>The one fix is to give every therapy a number or an end point the first time, because the paper won't probe you.

289
00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:20.982
<v Tutor>That's the deep dive.

290
00:23:20.982 --> 00:23:23.312
<v Tutor>Before Saturday, do one active thing.

291
00:23:23.312 --> 00:23:27.403
<v Tutor>From memory, write your five causes from five categories with examples.

292
00:23:27.403 --> 00:23:30.821
<v Tutor>Then write a one-line fluid plan with a rate and a target.

293
00:23:30.821 --> 00:23:36.983
<v Tutor>Check both against the show notes, and check the rhabdomyolysis section of Oh's chapter on acute kidney injury.

294
00:23:36.983 --> 00:23:42.990
<v Tutor>And on Friday, when your questions come back, give fluid the same weight as blood whenever a stem says both.

295
00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:44.440
<v Tutor>See you on Saturday.
